<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gomes Freire de Andrade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1685–1763</span> · Also known as Count of Bobadela</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gomes Freire de Andrade, Count of Bobadela, was a Portuguese general who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> from 1733 to 1763, at times also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> and much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. He reorganised gold taxation and built public works such as the Carioca <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct</a>, and commanded the Guaraní War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1750.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gomes-freire-de-andrade.jpg" alt="Gomes Freire de Andrade" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roque Gameiro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gomes_Freire_de_Andrade_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
